# UI Layer

React is the delivery mechanism. Keep it thin.

## Allowed in ui/

- Components, pages, layouts
- Hooks that adapt use cases to React state (loading, error, data)
- Providers for dependency injection
- Styles and assets (`ui/assets/`)

## Forbidden in ui/

- Business rules, validations, or domain calculations
- Direct API calls or repository implementations
- Importing from `@core/infrastructure/**`

```typescript
// ✅ GOOD — hook delegates to use case
const { getUsers } = useAppContext()
const users = await getUsers.execute()

// ❌ BAD — business logic in UI
if (user.age < 18) throw new Error('Too young')
```

## Hooks

- Prefix with `use` (`useUsers`, `useCreateUser`)
- Handle only presentation concerns: state, effects, event handlers, entity → view model mapping
- Get use cases from `AppProvider` / context, not `new GetUsers()`

## Component structure (index + ui)

Each visual component lives in its own folder with two files:

```
ComponentName/
├── index.tsx   # wrapper: hooks, state, handlers, passes props
└── ui.tsx      # presentational: props + JSX only, no use cases
```

- **`index.tsx`**: smart wrapper, imports hook/context, handles loading/error
- **`ui.tsx`**: dumb component, no `@core` imports, no business logic

```typescript
// index.tsx
export function UsersPage() {
  const { users, loading, error } = useUsers()
  if (loading) return <p>Loading...</p>
  return <UsersUi users={users} />
}

// ui.tsx — no hooks, no use cases
export function UsersUi({ users }: UsersUiProps) { ... }
```

## Components

- Prefer function components
- Pages compose components and hooks; no domain logic or validations
